      "dom_id": "question-112878",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{5}-5{x}^{3}+6?$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $6$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }6}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $4$ does not take the above form. The value $4$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{4}{1},$ and the numerator $(4)$ is not a factor of $6.$",
